# create-inside-try, narrowed (Task 9a): fires ONLY when the try's own
# finally block actually frees the just-constructed variable.
#
# P (genuine hazard -- rule fires): line 21 constructs SL as the first
# protected statement, and the SAME try's finally at line 24 frees SL. NOT on
# the correct pattern where the object is created before the try (line 26)
# and the try's first statement is SL.Add (line 27/28).
create-inside-try 21
!create-inside-try 26
!create-inside-try 27
!create-inside-try 28
#
# Q (YADF 5483 shape -- no finding): Sb is constructed at line 46 as the
# first statement of the OUTER try, but that try's finally (line 53) frees
# Root, not Sb -- Sb has its own nested try..finally (Sb.Free at line 50)
# immediately after. The outer finally never touches an undefined Sb.
!create-inside-try 46
#
# R (finally frees a different var -- no finding): A is constructed at line
# 66 as the first statement of the try, but the try's finally (line 69)
# frees B, a different, already-assigned variable -- not A.
!create-inside-try 66
#
# Fix 4 (2026-08-11 review): the lhs gate used to require a bare identifier,
# so an indexed or multi-level qualified target was silently dropped even
# when the same finally demonstrably freed the exact target. All four below
# are new coverage for that widening.
#
# S (indexed lhs, finally frees the SAME element -- fires): line 87
# constructs Arr[0] as the try's first statement, and the same try's finally
# (line 89) frees Arr[0].
create-inside-try 87
#
# T (indexed lhs, finally frees a DIFFERENT element -- no finding): line 102
# constructs Arr[0] as the try's first statement, but the try's finally
# (line 104) frees Arr[1], not Arr[0].
!create-inside-try 102
#
# U (multi-level lhs 'A.B.C', finally frees the SAME target -- fires): line
# 116 constructs Outer.Inner.Obj as the try's first statement, and the same
# try's finally (line 118) frees Outer.Inner.Obj via the identical path.
create-inside-try 116
#
# V (multi-level lhs, finally frees a DIFFERENT qualified target -- no
# finding): line 132 constructs Outer1.Inner.Obj as the try's first
# statement, but the try's finally (line 134) frees Outer2.Inner.Obj --
# textually different.
!create-inside-try 132
